+that reference, or taken from a field place (`&raw const o.h`), is `__rt.box(object)`, or
+`__rt.box_chain(object, chain)` when the type has a zero-offset chain (below). A reborrow of a raw
+pointer, `&*p as *const T`, is `p` itself; the compiler recognizes it from how the reference was
+made, never from how it is spelled, because a reference *loaded* through a pointer to a pointer is
+spelled `pp.buf[pp.off]` as well. Equality and addresses read the object, never the box buffer
+(which carries `$bx`, so telling the two apart is one property read), so `&o.h as *const H`,
+`&raw const o.h` and the erased `(&o.h as *const H).cast::<()>()` are one identity and one address.
